winning top honors in the documentary  category this year's director keiko  wright with her film hero  are stories of japanese internment  told through the eyes of her grandfather  the film made at new york university  documents the political hysteria racism  and scars evoked by japanese internment  during world war ii  first off i'd like to thank the academy  for this honor this week has been  absolutely amazing i have been telling  people i've been like a kid in a candy  store we have seen so many amazing  things and the people we have talked to  have been so generous with their time  and their  knowledge on the craft  i'd like to thank my family for all  their support i would like to thank my  crew  especially  reika shankar and jamie lee  as well as everyone who donated their  time and their talent  i would also like to thank my mentor at  nyu marsha moore who without whom i  would not be here tonight she was the  one who pushed me to enter the film  into  into here  lastly i would like to thank my  grandfather who  bravely shared his story with me and  allowed me to make this film  which i hope reminds all of us how  ever vigilant we have to be in  protecting each other's human and civil  rights and i'd like to thank you again  for this honor and i hope you guys all  check out everyone's films they're  amazing and keep up with all these  filmmakers because they're going to be  making amazing projects so thank you
 

